<p>The BRRRR method stands for buy, rehab, rent, refinance, repeat. You buy a property below its potential value, renovate it, rent it out, then refinance based on the new, higher value to pull out most or all of the cash you put in, and use that cash on the next deal. When it works, you build a rental portfolio while recycling the same pool of capital.</p>
<p>When it does not, you end up with money trapped in a property that barely covers its mortgage. The difference is almost always in the numbers you run before you buy.</p>

<h2>How the BRRRR method works, step by step</h2>
<h3>1. Buy below value</h3>
<p>Your profit is made at purchase. BRRRR investors look for distressed or dated properties that sellers want to unload quickly: estate sales, tired landlords, homes needing major repairs, and properties that cannot get conventional financing in their current condition. In Houston, that can include homes with storm or water damage and sellers who want to <a href="/blog/selling-a-house-as-is-texas/">sell a house as-is</a>.</p>
<p>Because these houses often will not qualify for a standard mortgage, investors usually buy with cash, a hard money loan or private money.</p>
<h3>2. Rehab for rent and appraisal</h3>
<p>Renovate to the level of the rental market, not to your personal taste. Focus first on systems and anything an appraiser or tenant notices: roof, HVAC, plumbing, electrical, foundation, flooring, kitchens and bathrooms. Durable finishes such as luxury vinyl plank flooring hold up better between tenants.</p>
<p>Pull permits where required and keep every invoice and photo. You will want that record for the appraiser and for your own depreciation and tax records.</p>
<h3>3. Rent it to a qualified tenant</h3>
<p>A signed lease at market rent matters for two reasons. It starts your cash flow, and many refinance lenders, especially DSCR lenders, size the loan partly on the rent. Screen carefully: a bad tenant in the first year can wipe out the savings from the whole strategy.</p>
<h3>4. Refinance into long-term debt</h3>
<p>Once the property is rehabbed and rented, you refinance with a long-term mortgage based on the new appraised value. The goal is a loan large enough to repay the short-term financing and return your cash. Seasoning rules and loan-to-value caps, covered below, determine how much you can actually pull out and when.</p>
<h3>5. Repeat</h3>
<p>Recovered cash funds the next purchase. Each cycle adds a property and a mortgage, so your systems for management, bookkeeping and reserves need to scale along with the portfolio.</p>

<h2>The 70% rule: how much to pay</h2>
<p>The classic screen for a BRRRR or flip purchase is the 70% rule:</p>
<blockquote><p>Maximum purchase price = (after-repair value x 70%) - repair costs</p></blockquote>
<p>The 30% cushion has to cover purchase and refinance closing costs, hard money points and interest, taxes and insurance during the rehab, utilities, overruns and your margin of safety. Many BRRRR investors find that the 70% rule is roughly what it takes to leave little or no cash in the deal after a refinance at about 75% of value.</p>
<p>The weak spots are the inputs. After-repair value (ARV) should come from recent sales of renovated, comparable homes nearby, ideally pulled by a local agent. Repair estimates should come from contractor bids, plus a contingency of 10% to 20% for what you find behind the walls.</p>

[[product:moisture-meter]]

<h2>Refinance rules: seasoning, LTV and DSCR loans</h2>
<h3>Conventional cash-out refinance</h3>
<p>Under Fannie Mae's Selling Guide, a cash-out refinance generally requires at least one borrower to have been on title for at least six months. On top of that, if the refinance pays off an existing first mortgage, that mortgage must be at least 12 months old, measured from note date to note date. For BRRRR investors using a hard money loan, that 12-month rule can effectively rule out a conventional cash-out refinance in the first year.</p>
<p>Fannie Mae also has a delayed financing exception for properties bought with cash and no mortgage. It allows a cash-out refinance within six months, but the new loan is limited to your documented purchase investment plus allowed closing costs, and all other cash-out rules still apply. That may not return the money you spent on the rehab.</p>
<h3>DSCR loans</h3>
<p>Debt service coverage ratio (DSCR) loans are non-QM investor mortgages that qualify the property mainly on its rent. The lender divides rent by the full payment including principal, interest, taxes, insurance and any HOA dues. Requirements vary widely by lender, but as of 2026 many lenders look for:</p>
<ul>
<li>A DSCR of roughly 1.0 to 1.25, though some go lower at a higher price</li>
<li>Credit scores in roughly the 620 to 700 range, with better pricing at higher scores</li>
<li>Cash-out loan-to-value caps commonly around 70% to 75%</li>
<li>Seasoning rules that range from none to several months, sometimes with the loan capped at cost until a period passes</li>
</ul>
<h3>A Texas note on cash-out loans</h3>
<p>Texas has strict constitutional rules on home equity cash-out loans, including an 80% loan-to-value cap, but those rules apply to homesteads. A rental you do not live in is generally not subject to them, which is why investor cash-out refinances in Texas work much as they do elsewhere. Make sure the property is not carrying a homestead exemption, and confirm the treatment with your lender and title company.</p>
<h3>Help the appraisal</h3>
<p>Give the appraiser a one-page summary of the work done, with costs, permits and before-and-after photos, plus recent sales of renovated comparable homes. You cannot tell an appraiser what value to reach, but you can make sure they see everything you improved.</p>
<p>DSCR loans usually carry higher rates than conventional loans and often have prepayment penalties. Get the term sheet in writing before you buy, not after the rehab, and confirm current terms with a licensed lender.</p>

<h2>Worked example: a Houston-area BRRRR</h2>
<p>Here is a hypothetical three-bedroom house. All numbers are illustrative assumptions, not market data, so replace them with real bids, comps and quotes.</p>
<ul>
<li>After-repair value: $220,000; rehab budget: $40,000</li>
<li>70% rule maximum: ($220,000 x 0.70) - $40,000 = $114,000</li>
