<h3>What this example shows</h3>
<p>The first column is the BRRRR dream: nearly all your cash comes back. But the property passes the lender's DSCR test while losing money every month once you budget for vacancy, repairs and management. A DSCR above 1.0 is not the same as positive cash flow.</p>
<p>Leaving $27,000 in the deal improves things, but self-managed cash flow is only about $78 a month, roughly a 3.5% cash-on-cash return. At higher interest rates, with Texas property taxes and insurance, many BRRRR deals in the Houston area look like this. They make sense only if you buy at a deeper discount, find a higher rent-to-value property, or accept leaving more money in. Run your own deals through our <a href="/tools/rental-property-calculator/">rental property calculator</a> and see our full guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-analyze-a-rental-property/">how to analyze a rental property</a>.</p>

<h2>BRRRR risks to plan for</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Low appraisal.</strong> If the property appraises below your ARV, your refinance shrinks and cash stays trapped. Buy with a margin and give the appraiser a list of comparable sales and improvements.</li>
<li><strong>Rehab overruns and delays.</strong> Every extra month on a hard money loan costs interest, and surprises like foundation repairs or old plumbing are common in older Houston homes.</li>
<li><strong>Rate changes.</strong> Your refinance rate is set months after you buy. A rate increase in between can turn a positive deal negative.</li>
<li><strong>Tax reappraisal.</strong> After renovation and sale, expect the appraisal district to raise the value toward the ARV. Budget taxes on the after-repair value, not the purchase price.</li>
<li><strong>Insurance.</strong> Vacant properties under renovation need a builder's risk or vacant dwelling policy, and landlord coverage afterward can be costly.</li>
<li><strong>Leverage stacking.</strong> Repeating the cycle with maximum leverage means several thin-margin properties. One bad year can strain the whole portfolio.</li>
</ol>
<p>The structure of your loans, entities and taxes affects your liability and returns, so review your plan with a real estate attorney and CPA.</p>

<h2>BRRRR vs. buying a turnkey rental</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>BRRRR</th><th>Turnkey rental</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Cash recovered</td><td>Some or most, if the refinance works</td><td>None until sale or a later refinance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Work required</td><td>High: sourcing, rehab, leasing, refinancing</td><td>Low</td></tr>
<tr><td>Financing</td><td>Short-term, then long-term; two closings</td><td>One long-term loan</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main risk</td><td>Appraisal, budget and timeline</td><td>Overpaying for retail condition</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>The BRRRR method can recycle your capital into multiple rentals, but only if you buy at a real discount, control the rehab and refinance into a loan the rent can actually support. Use the 70% rule as a ceiling, understand the 6- and 12-month conventional seasoning rules, get DSCR terms in writing early, and judge every deal on true cash flow after vacancy, repairs and management, not on the DSCR alone.</p>
